const REVIEWED = [{"from": "geometry review", "severity": "high", "title": "Opaque 400 m cells straddling the 800 m ring hide a Sun that the map, the mask and the callouts all call visible (breaks D7)", "file": "app/lib/core/lander_view.dart", "line": 541, "failure_scenario": "_cellRow drops a near cell only when all 4 corners are within 800 m. Cells that straddle the ring stay in part 1 and get alpha 1 (line 728). Their triangles have dmin < 800 m, so _skyChunk (line 572) leaves them out of the far skyline. That same skyline drives the near-field alpha (line 735) and skyBody's line-3 check. I measured this on the shipped DEM at the Shackleton rim preset (derosa2012-sr1-shackleton-rim). The 400 m eye is 29 m below the precise site height (1703.8 vs 1733.1 m). At az 80°, a ray at el 0.0 to +0.2° hits an opaque part-1 triangle about 290-300 m away (vertices at 283, 522 and 537 m horizontal), which rises to +0.33°. At that azimuth both the far skyline and horizons.json are -1.21°. Opaque near ground sits more than 0.1° above both over about 35° of azimuth at Shackleton rim (worst +1.54°) and about 40° at Mons Mouton (worst +0.62° at az 311°). The Sun circles the horizon within about ±1.6° at these sites, so with the Sun at az 80°, el -1.0° to +0.3°, the 3D view paints opaque terrain over its disc. Meanwhile the status line and the callout say 'in view', the 2D light map shows the site sunlit, and line 3 stays silent because the far skyline is -1.21°. This is the exact case D7 rules out ('Opaque near ground would hide a Sun that the map, the masks and the sky plot all call visible').", "suggested_fix": "Apply the lattice's see-through rule to the near ground that part 1 keeps. One option: in _finish, give every part-1 vertex with _dh < landerNearFieldM alpha landerNearAlpha when _el[v] > skylineAt(_sky, _az[v]). Another: drop part-1 cells with any corner inside 800 m and extend the lattice rings to about 800 + 400·√2 m. Add a test that the opaque (alpha 1) drawn skyline never exceeds max(far skyline, horizons.json) by more than a small tolerance at the presets.", "status": "confirmed", "verified_fix": "This is the smallest fix: apply the lattice's see-through rule to the part-1 vertices under 800 m. Those vertices are never in the far skyline, because any triangle that contains one has dmin < 800. So comparing them with the skyline is safe. Leave vertices at 800 m or more alone, so far peaks are not made translucent by skyline sampling between 0.5° steps.\n\nIn app/lib/core/lander_view.dart `_finish`, change\n```dart\n      part[o] = isFar ? 2 : 1;\n```\nto\n```dart\n      part[o] = isFar ? 2 : 1;\n      // D7: near ground the 400 m cells keep inside 800 m (cells straddling the ring) is see-through above the far skyline, like the lattice\n      if (!isFar && _dh[v] < landerNearFieldM && _el[v] > skylineAt(_sky, _az[v])) alpha[o] = landerNearAlpha;\n```\nThe check is placed before the pixel/centre split, so fan centres are covered too.\n\n**Tests and spec:**\n- Update docs/landers_view_spec.md §3.9 to read: \"a part 0 vertex, or a part 1 vertex under 800 m, gets alpha 1 when its el ≤ the far skyline, else 0.35\".\n- In app/test/core/lander_view_test.dart, 'near field: lattice, alpha and budget' (around line 415): apply the under/over check also to part 1 when hypot(e, n) < landerNearFieldM, and expect alpha 1 for the other part 1/2 vertices.\n- Add a shipped-DEM test for SR1, Mons Mouton, Haworth and the custom spot. For each azimuth, take the max elevation over edges of part 0/1 triangles whose vertices all have alpha 1. Assert it never exceeds the far skyline (`m.skyline`) by more than 0.2°.\n- The lander_light tests read `m.alpha` and need no change.", "evidence": "I reproduced this on the shipped DEM with a throwaway test in /home/user/lv-wt/app/test/_rev_tmp_v0/. The test is deleted and the worktree is clean. The worktree HEAD read ecd4cd4, not 6ee08b1, but the line numbers all match the report.\n\n**Mechanism, confirmed in /home/user/lv-wt/app/lib/core/lander_view.dart:**\n- `_cellRow` (around line 539) skips a part-1 cell only when all 4 corners have `_dh <= 800`. Cells that straddle the ring are kept.\n- `_skyChunk` (line 572) leaves out every triangle with `_triDmin < 800`. The straddling triangles are therefore not in the far skyline `_sky`.\n- `_finish` sets alpha 1 for every vertex (line 728). The see-through rule (line 735) runs only in the `v < _nL` branch, i.e. part 0. Part-1 vertices, including fan centres and corners under 800 m, always stay alpha 1.\n- The painter (app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart, `paint()`) draws the Sun and Earth discs first. The terrain is drawn over them (\"the terrain then hides both\").\n- `skyBody` (core/lander_view.dart around line 963) judges the Sun against `m.skyline`, which is the far skyline that leaves out these triangles.\n\n**Measurement (meshSkyline(m, minDistM: 0, parts: {1}) on the culled mesh, compared with m.skyline and horizons.json at 2 m):**\n- **Shackleton rim:** opaque part-1 ground is more than 0.1° above max(far, mask) over 35.0° of azimuth. The worst point is +1.54° at az 80: part-1 terrain at +0.33°, far skyline -1.21°, mask -1.21°. It is more than 0.1° above the far skyline alone over 105.5° (worst +2.43° at az 171.5).\n- **Mons Mouton:** more than 0.1° above max(far, mask) over 40.5°. The worst point is +0.62° at az 311: part 1 at 3.78°, far 1.28°, mask 3.16°. Above the far skyline alone: 152.5°, worst +3.85°.\n- **Haworth:** 0° above max(far, mask), but 110.5° above the far skyline (worst +1.74°).\n- **Custom spot (-80.05, 1.44):** 136° above the far skyline (worst +2.32°).\n\nSo at Shackleton rim, with the Sun at az 80 and el between about -1.0° and +0.3°, `skyBody` says \"up\" because the skyline there is -1.21°. Meanwhile the disc is painted under opaque terrain. That is the case D7 rules out.\n\n**The suggested fix, simulated on the same mesh:**\n- Change: part-1 vertices with `_dh < 800` that sit above the far skyline get alpha 0.35. This touches only 4 to 8 vertices per site.\n- Fully opaque (alpha 1) excess at Shackleton: from 35°/+1.54° to 0°/+0.03° over max(far, mask).\n- At Mons Mouton: from 40.5°/+0.62° to 0°/0.00°.\n- Over the far skyline alone, worst is at most 0."}, {"from": "geometry review", "severity": "low", "title": "A coarse far frame stays on screen next to a full near frame, unlabelled (stale check ignores `coarse`)", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 269, "failure_scenario": "_recolor (line 269), the probe (343) and the caption (509) show the far frame whenever its tMs equals the near frame's. §2.4 also requires `coarse` to match. On native, while playing, the far frame at T is computed on the 1.6 km grid. Pausing at T replaces the near frame with the full 400 m frame. Its tMs is still T, so the 1.6 km far light keeps showing beyond 64 km, yet the 'Approximate: while playing…' badge is gone because it keys on the near frame's coarse flag. If the full far compute then fails (the catch only clears _farSent), the unlabelled coarse light stays indefinitely. CLAUDE.md says any approximation is labelled.", "suggested_fix": "Compare `_far!.request.coarse == near.request.coarse` together with tMs in all three places, ideally through one shared getter, so a mismatched far frame drops to 'light not computed' grey until the matching one lands.", "status": "not verified", "verified_fix": null, "evidence": null}, {"from": "geometry review", "severity": "low", "title": "Far frame eclipse uses observer height 0 m, not the site's terrain height", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 293, "failure_scenario": "_syncFar calls lightFrameEclipse(raw) with the default terrainHeightM = 0. The near frame passes through light_map._drawn with site.terrainHeightM, and §4.3 requires the same for both frames. At Mons Mouton (6397 m) the far frame's eclipse observer is 6.4 km lower, which shifts Earth's apparent position by about 3 arcsec. For a few seconds around each eclipse contact the two frames then disagree: for example the near frame is total (Sun bits cleared, ground dark) while the far frame is partial (Sun bits kept), so ground beyond 64 km is drawn sunlit next to dark near ground at the same moment.", "suggested_fix": "Use `lightFrameEclipse(raw, terrainHeightM: _s.site.terrainHeightM)` (the far request's lat/lon is the site), or reuse the SunEclipse already computed for the shown near frame at the same tMs.", "status": "not verified", "verified_fix": null, "evidence": null}, {"from": "ui review", "severity": "high", "title": "Phone landscape: lander panel overflows its square box; the view shrinks to 120 dp and the buttons and notes are clipped", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 528, "failure_scenario": "In phone landscape (LightMap._short layout), light_map.dart:994 passes width = maxHeight = side, and side is usually below 336 dp (for example about 250 dp on a 915x412 phone). With width < 336 the seven buttons wrap to 2 rows (96 dp), and the 4-line notes need about 100 dp. viewH = max(120, ...) then hits its 120 floor, and the Column needs 120 + 4 + 96 + notes, which is more than side. A throwaway test with LanderViewPanel(width: 210, maxHeight: 210) in a 210-dp box gave 'A RenderFlex overflowed by 118 pixels on the bottom', with the view 210x120. In release, the button row (all 48 dp controls, including Face the Sun) and the caption are cut off. Spec §7.2 says the view takes the full height in phone landscape. The existing overflow test only covers 360 and 1280 inside a SingleChildScrollView, so it never sets maxHeight.", "suggested_fix": "When maxHeight is finite and the side-by-side short layout applies, either give the panel the full row width (the view wide and full height, with the buttons as one row or an overlay column) or put the notes and buttons in the side column. At minimum, wrap the panel body in a SingleChildScrollView when 120 + 4 + 48*rows + notesH > maxHeight. Add a widget test at width = maxHeight = 250 asserting no exception.", "status": "confirmed", "verified_fix": "A small fix in app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart only. I verified it: all 5 repro cases had no exception, and test/widget/lander_view_test.dart passed 16/16. I then reverted it, so the worktree is clean.\n\n(1) At line 528, change `final viewH` to `var viewH` and add these lines after it:\n```dart\nfinal scroll = widget.maxHeight.isFinite && viewH + 4 + 48.0 * rows + notesH > widget.maxHeight;\nif (scroll) viewH = math.max(0.0, math.min(viewH, widget.maxHeight - 4 - 48));\n```\n\n(2) Replace the returned SizedBox/Column (lines 674-682) with this:\n```dart\nfinal below = [buttons, for (final n in notes) Text(n, style: PHText.small.copyWith(color: PH.muted), maxLines: 4, overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis)];\nreturn SizedBox(width: w, height: scroll ? widget.maxHeight : null,\n  child: Column(mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min, crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.stretch, children: [\n    ClipRRect(borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(PH.radiusInner), child: SizedBox(height: viewH, child: view)),\n    const SizedBox(height: 4),\n    if (scroll) Expanded(child: SingleChildScrollView(child: Column(crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.stretch, children: below))) else ...below,\n  ]));\n```\n\nThis removes the overflow. The first button row, with Face the Sun, always stays on screen, and the rest of the buttons and the notes scroll under the view. The scroll is only around the buttons and notes, so it does not compete with the view's scale gesture. Portrait and wide layouts (infinite maxHeight) are unchanged.\n\n(3) Add a regression test in test/widget/lander_view_test.dart. Pump LightTab in the 851x393 window with the 771x290 slot, and in the 891x411 window with the 735x228 slot. Tap 'Lander', call meshReady, then assert `tester.takeException()` is null and that the 'Face the Sun' tooltip rect lies inside the LanderViewPanel rect.\n\nOptional, separate from the fix: to get closer to §7.2 (the view at full height), the short layout in light_map.dart could give the lander panel a width of c.maxWidth - 16 - 240 instead of side. The buttons then fit in one row. The scroll fallback above is still needed for the 172-dp-tall case.", "evidence": "The claim holds. I reproduced it with the real LightTab, not only a panel in an artificial box. The worktree HEAD is ecd4cd4, not 6ee08b1.\n\nCode path:\n- In phone landscape, light_tab.dart takes the `LightMap.shortSideBySideFor` branch.\n- In that layout, light_map.dart:1182 sets side = min(c.maxHeight, c.maxWidth - 256), and side is limited by the height.\n- light_map.dart:994 then passes `LanderViewPanel(width: side, maxHeight: side)`. Because it sits in a Row with `crossAxisAlignment.start`, the panel gets a loose height limit of c.maxHeight.\n- In lander_view.dart, line 505 gives rows = 2 whenever w < 336 (`buttonsWidth` = 7 × 48). Line 528 clamps viewH to at least 120.\n- The Column at lines 674-682 needs viewH + 4 + 96 + notesH. Nothing there scrolls.\n\nThrowaway test (since deleted): LightTab with the fake engine and synthetic DEM from lander_view_test, using the window and slot sizes already in light_tab_test.dart, then tapping the 'Lander' segment:\n- 851x393 window, 771x290 slot: panel 234x234, view 234x120, \"A RenderFlex overflowed by 58 pixels on the bottom.\"\n- 891x411 window (the Android 2340x1080 case), 735x228 slot: panel 172x172, \"overflowed by 168 pixels\".\n- The panel alone in a Row at side 172, 234 and 250 overflowed by 204, 94 and 78 px.\n\nSo in landscape the view is stuck at 120 dp and does not take the full height that spec §7.2 asks for. The notes are cut off, and in the 172 box the whole second button row (Zoom in, Turn right 15°) is also cut off.\n\nOne overstatement in the report: Face the Sun is in the first button row (y 124-172), so it stays visible in every case I measured. The existing test at 360 and 1280 never sets a finite maxHeight, which is why it misses this."}, {"from": "ui review", "severity": "medium", "title": "Canvas text (centre probe, callouts) is single-line and clipped at 320/360 px and text x2; caption ellipsised already at 400 dp", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 1047, "failure_scenario": "Widths measured with the bundled Plex fonts: the probe 'Centre: full Sun · 12.4 km · az 131.0° el +0.8°' is 310 px at x1 and 620 px at x2. 'Centre: Sun and Earth · …' is 343 px and 686 px. Callout line 1 'Sun · az 281.7° · el +0.5°' is 343 px at x2. Line 3 '400 m terrain here: partly hidden, skyline +0.2°' is 478 px at x2. On a 360 dp phone the view is about 316 dp wide (about 276 dp at 320). The probe box is clipped to size.width-12 (line 1047), so at x1 the 'el' value is already cut, and at x2 everything after the distance is lost. canvas text is clamped only at PH.maxCanvasTextScale = 2.0. In calloutBox's last fallback, callouts wider than the view are drawn from x=0 and run off the right edge. The notes under the view use maxLines: 4 with an ellipsis (line 680). The screenshots at 400 dp x1 already show the caption truncated mid-word: '…the Sun and Earth are at their exact p…'. At x2 the caption loses most of its honesty text.", "suggested_fix": "Lay out canvas texts with layout(maxWidth: view width - margins) and allow wrapping, or split the probe into two lines (class · distance / az · el). For callouts, cap the width and wrap, falling back to a shorter line 3 such as '400 m: hidden, +0.2°'. Remove maxLines/ellipsis from the notes and caption, or keep it only in the landscape budget and expand there. Add a 320 px x2 widget test.", "status": "confirmed", "verified_fix": "Smallest fix, all in app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart: let canvas labels wrap to the view width, and stop truncating the notes when the panel has no height budget.\n\n1. In `_text` (l.733), add `double maxWidth = double.infinity`, include it in the cache key (`'$s|$size|…|$maxWidth'`), and call `..layout(maxWidth: maxWidth)` instead of `..layout()`. With maxLines unset, TextPainter wraps, and `t.width <= maxWidth`.\n\n2. Probe (l.1046-1047):\n   - `final t = _text(msg, size: 11, mono: status == null, maxWidth: size.width - 20);`\n   - `final r = Rect.fromLTWH(6, bottom - t.height - 4, t.width + 8, t.height + 4);`\n   - The clipRect can stay; it no longer cuts anything. At x2 the box grows to 2-3 lines upward from `bottom`.\n\n3. Callouts (l.927): `_text(l, size: 11, color: …, mono: i == 0, maxWidth: v.w - 6)`. Then `box.width <= v.w`, and calloutBox's fallback keeps the box inside the view. Optionally shorten line 3 to `'400 m: $mesh, ${sgn(sky)}'` so the box stays short at x2 during an eclipse; otherwise clampY pins a box taller than the view to the top.\n\n4. Mask label (l.1025): `_text(maskLabel, size: 10, color: PH.text, maxWidth: size.width - 40)`, so `box.left >= 6`.\n\n5. Notes (l.680, and the measurement at l.522-523): use `maxLines: widget.maxHeight.isFinite ? 4 : null` and `overflow: widget.maxHeight.isFinite ? TextOverflow.ellipsis : null` on the Text, and the same maxLines in the notesH TextPainter.\n   - Portrait phones pass `maxHeight: double.infinity` (light_map.dart:994), so they show the full caption and honesty text.\n   - The finite landscape budget keeps the 4-line cap, so viewH cannot collapse.\n\n6. Test: in lander_view_test.dart, add a 320x1000 case at textScaleFactorTestValue = 2 with `panel(width: 320)`. It should check:\n   - Every note's RenderParagraph has `didExceedMaxLines == false`.\n   - With the painter's textScaler, `LanderPainter.calloutBox(v, a, Size(tw + 6, th + 2), …)` lies within [0, v.w] after the wrap (or expose the probe rect through a small static helper and assert it lies inside the view).\n   - `tester.takeException()` is null.\n\nDo not change the science, colours or fonts.", "evidence": "The claim holds. Checked in the worktree /home/user/lv-wt (HEAD shows ecd4cd4) with the bundled IBM Plex fonts (loadPlexFonts). I ran two throwaway tests: a text measure, and a copy of lander_view_test.dart with the real LanderViewPanel, fake engine and synthetic DEM. Both were deleted afterwards and the tree is clean.\n\nCode (app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart):\n- `_text()` (l.733-746) always calls `layout()` with no maxWidth, so every canvas label is a single line of unlimited width. The canvas text scale is only clamped to `PH.maxCanvasTextScale = 2.0` (l.552).\n- Probe (l.1046-1051): the box is `Rect.fromLTWH(6, …, min(t.width, size.width-12)+8, …)` and the canvas is then clipped to that box. Text wider than the view is cut silently, with no ellipsis.\n- Callouts (l.927-929): the box is `Size(tw+6, th+2)` with tw unbounded. When the box is wider than the view, the last fallback in calloutBox (l.890) places it at `left = max(0, min(.., v.w - box.width))` = 0, so it runs past the right edge and the full-view clip cuts it.\n- Notes and caption: `maxLines: 4, overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis` (l.680; the measurement at l.522 matches it).\n\nMeasured widths (mono 11 px):\n| Text | x1 | x2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 'Centre: full Sun · 12.4 km · az 131.0° el +0.8°' | 310 | 620 |\n| 'Centre: Sun and Earth · …' | 343 | 686 |\n| 'Centre: light not computed · …' | 376 | 752 |\n| Callout 'Sun · az 281.7° · el +0.5°' | 172 | 343 |\n| '400 m terrain here: partly hidden, skyline +0.2°' (sans) | 239 | 478 |\n| Eclipse line 2 | 261 | 521 |\n\nReal widget repro (view 316x220, and 360x223):\n- **316 at x1:** the caption Text has didExceedMaxLines = true, so it is ellipsised.\n- **316 at x2:** the probe 'Centre: sky · az 118.1° el +0.0°' is 422 px, but only 300 px of room is visible. The callout 'Earth · az 156.5° · el +5.8°' box is 376 px wide on a 316 px view, so the el value is cut off.\n- **360 at x2:** the probe (422 vs 344) and the Earth callout (376 vs 360) are still clipped, and the caption is ellipsised.\n\nBecause the text is clipped rather than ellipsised, a number can be cut mid-value (for example 'az 13' for 131.0°). The existing 360 dp x2 test only checks for exceptions, so it passes anyway.\n\nOne part of the claim did not reproduce: the caption at 400 dp x1 fits in 4 lines with 'Shackleton rim'. It depends on the site name and UTC string length.\n\nSame root cause, not measured (estimated about 380 px at x2): the mask-reference label at l.1025-1026 has `box.left = size.width - t.width - 34"}, {"from": "ui review", "severity": "medium", "title": "Lunar eclipse: Sun and Earth callouts drawn on top of each other (calloutBox fallback ignores the other callout)", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 886, "failure_scenario": "mp1_eclipse.png (MP1, 2026-03-03 11:30 UTC, Both layer, facing the Sun; captured after the calloutBox change) shows the 3-line Sun callout and the 2-line Earth callout overlapping, so 'hidden by Earth (lunar eclipse)' and the 400 m line cannot be read. The Sun box takes candidate 3 (centred under its disc). For Earth (avoid = Sun box), right and left are outside the view or hit Earth's own disc, and candidate 3 overlaps the Sun box. Candidate 4 (above the glyph) sits about 2 px into the compass strip (top < compassH+1), so it fails inside(). The second loop then returns candidate 3 without checking `avoid`, which puts it right over the Sun box. Coincident bodies are exactly the eclipse case the feature highlights.", "suggested_fix": "In the fallback, before ignoring `avoid`, try stacking directly below or above the other box (Rect at avoid.bottom+4 or avoid.top-4-h, clamped to the view). Or, when the two anchors are within a few degrees, merge both bodies into one callout box (Sun lines then Earth lines). Add the eclipse geometry (same az, el 3.9 vs 3.2, rPx 7 vs 25, view 356x330) to the calloutBox unit test.", "status": "confirmed", "verified_fix": "In `LanderPainter.calloutBox` (app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart, around line 875), add two stacked candidates after the existing four. They sit directly below or above the other callout's box, centred on this body's glyph, so the first loop tries them with the full `clear()` check before the fallback loop ignores `avoid`:\n\n    final cands = [\n      Rect.fromLTWH(a.glyph.dx + 11, top, box.width, box.height),\n      Rect.fromLTWH(a.glyph.dx - 11 - box.width, top, box.width, box.height),\n      Rect.fromLTWH(cx, a.disc.dy + a.rPx + 6, box.width, box.height),\n      Rect.fromLTWH(cx, a.glyph.dy - 14 - box.height, box.width, box.height),\n      // stacked against the other callout (a lunar eclipse puts both bodies at one spot)\n      if (avoid != null) ...[\n        Rect.fromLTWH(cx, avoid.bottom + 4, box.width, box.height),\n        Rect.fromLTWH(cx, avoid.top - 4 - box.height, box.width, box.height),\n      ],\n    ];\n\nAlso add 'else stacked below/above the other callout' to the doc comment.\n\nI checked this fix with the same throwaway test:\n- 356x220, hfov 20.5: Earth box moves to LTRB(89.5, 161.8, 266.5, 191.8). No overlap with the Sun box or the Earth disc, and it stays inside the view.\n- hfov 45: Earth box at LTRB(89.5, 136.8, 266.5, 166.8), no overlap.\n\nExisting expectations are unchanged: the first four candidates and the avoid==null paths are the same, and the existing 'other' case still resolves to candidate 3.\n\nRegression test to add to the §5 calloutBox test in app/test/widget/lander_view_test.dart:\n\n    v = LanderView(yawDeg: 6.9, pitchDeg: 3.5, hfovDeg: 20.5, w: 356, h: 220)\n    sa = calloutAnchor(v, Sun el 3.9, rho 0.27)\n    ea = calloutAnchor(v, Earth el 3.2, rho 0.95)\n    sBox = calloutBox(v, sa, Size(210, 44), null, ...)\n    eBox = calloutBox(v, ea, Size(177, 30), sBox, ...)\n\nThen expect: eBox does not overlap sBox; eBox does not overlap the Earth disc rect; eBox.top >= compassH.", "evidence": "The claim holds. I checked it against the worktree at HEAD ecd4cd4 (the commit that added `LanderPainter.calloutBox`; line 886 is the return in the second fallback loop) and against the screenshot /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-CLPS-MOON-BROWSER/3f8ae6b1-fba8-5587-b499-4c90b54bab61/scratchpad/landers_shots/mp1_eclipse.png. The screenshot is 600x1500 at a device pixel ratio of 1.5, so the 48 dp compass strip is 72 px and the view is 356x220 dp. In it, the 3-line Sun box ('hidden by Earth (lunar eclipse)' plus the 400 m line) and the 2-line Earth box are drawn on top of each other and cannot be read.\n\nMechanism, from the code: `_callout` (line 1033) places the Sun first with avoid=null, then the Earth with avoid=sunBox. On a 356 dp wide view the eclipse text boxes are too wide for both side candidates, so the Sun takes candidate 3, centred under its disc. The Earth's anchor has the same x. Its candidate 1 is 189+177=366 > 356, candidate 2 has left < 0, candidate 3 overlaps the Sun box, and candidate 4 has top 85-14-30 = 41 < compassH+1 = 49, so `inside()` fails. The second loop then returns candidate 3 without checking `avoid`. The reviewer's '2 px' figure is about 8 px, but the conclusion is the same.\n\nReproduction: a throwaway flutter test (now deleted) used the real `LanderPainter.calloutAnchor` and `calloutBox` with Sun (az 6.9, el 3.9, rho 0.27) and Earth (az 6.9, el 3.2, rho 0.95). Box sizes were measured from the screenshot: Sun 210x44, Earth 177x30.\n- 356x220 view, hfov 20.5, pitch 3.5: Sun box LTRB(73, 113.8, 283, 157.8), Earth box LTRB(89.5, 137.7, 266.5, 167.7), overlap=true. Scaled by 1.5 this matches where the boxes sit in the screenshot.\n- Default hfov 45, pitch 0: overlap=true as well.\n- Taller views (330 or 260 dp): no overlap, because candidate 4 above the glyph fits.\n\nSo the bug shows on the phone layout at default zoom, during the eclipse that the feature calls out."}, {"from": "ui review", "severity": "low", "title": "Semantics label is stale and never states the view's status (building / computing / failed) after a site switch", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 561, "failure_scenario": "The label is _semantic, refreshed only in _recolor/_settled. Building the view…, Computing light… and 'The view could not be built: …' are painted on the canvas only. A throwaway test switched from the near-pole preset to another site with a failing DEM. Before: 'Lander's view, facing the Sun, azimuth 118 degrees…'. After: 'Lander's view, facing south-east, azimuth 118 degrees. Sun 4.6 degrees up, azimuth 345 degrees…'. That text was built in didUpdateWidget before _initCamera turned the camera to the new site's Sun (345°), so it names the wrong facing. It never says the view could not be built. On a successful switch the same stale facing is announced until the uncached mesh lands (up to about 1.2 s on the web).", "suggested_fix": "Call _refreshText() after _initCamera changes the camera, and from _ensureMesh when it resets _mesh or sets _meshError. Have describeLanderView (or the panel) prepend the status string when status != null, for example 'Lander's view: building the view…' / '…: the view could not be built.'. Add a W test for the site switch and error states.", "status": "not verified", "verified_fix": null, "evidence": null}, {"from": "performance review", "severity": "high", "title": "Mesh build blocks the UI thread: two single-unit phases of 120-165 ms, slices of about 20 ms, 2.3 s in total (budget 250 ms native / 1.2 s web, no slice over 8 ms)", "file": "app/lib/core/lander_view.dart", "line": 438, "failure_scenario": "Open the lander's view at SR1 (or switch site or observer height while in it). LanderViewPanelState._ensureMesh (widgets/lander_view.dart:232) runs LanderMeshWork on the UI thread with runSliced(rows: 8, sliceMs: 8). I measured it with a throwaway test on the shipped 400 m DEM (VM JIT, warmed up). The build took 2335 ms of CPU over 675 step(8) calls, and 86 of those calls each took more than 8 ms (typically 20-25 ms, because 8 units of the far-patch cell rows or the 4096-triangle cull/sky chunks run before the time check). Measured one unit at a time, two units are monolithic: _sortSweep() at 164 ms and _finish() at 120 ms. On the web build (dart2js, UI-thread engine) those become long tasks of several hundred ms that freeze the page while 'Building the view…' shows, and the whole build takes several seconds. Android also builds on the UI thread, so the time bar and map drop frames for seconds after every site change. The custom spot (-80.05, 1.44) behaves the same: 1.94 s, with 123 ms and 61 ms single units.", "suggested_fix": "Split _sortSweep (the radix passes and the heap set-up) and _finish (remap, compaction, the final radix sort, the sector fill) into chunked units of no more than about 4096 elements. Call runSliced with rows: 1, or make LanderMeshWork.step time-bounded, so that no slice goes past 8 ms. On native, consider building the mesh in Isolate.run (as dem_load_io.dart already does for the DEM) and returning the typed arrays through Isolate.exit. Also add a test that checks the largest single-unit time against the budget.", "status": "confirmed", "verified_fix": "The smallest correct fix keeps the results bit-identical, and the existing 'sliced build equals one-shot' test still covers that.\n\n(1) In core/lander_view.dart, replace the one-shot `_radix` with a resumable class whose units each handle at most 65,536 elements. Per 16-bit pass the units are: histogram chunks, then one prefix-sum unit, then scatter chunks, then a buffer swap and a cnt reset. For example:\n\n```\nclass _Radix {\n  final Uint32List keys;\n  Int32List src, dst;\n  final cnt = Int32List(65537);\n  int pass = 0, stage = 0, i = 0;\n  _Radix(Int32List ids, this.keys) : src = ids, dst = Int32List(ids.length);\n  bool get done => pass == 2;\n  void unit() {\n    final s = 16 * pass, n = src.length;\n    if (stage == 0) {\n      final e = math.min(n, i + 65536);\n      for (; i < e; i++) cnt[((keys[src[i]] >> s) & 0xFFFF) + 1]++;\n      if (i == n) { stage = 1; i = 0; }\n    } else if (stage == 1) {\n      for (var k = 1; k < 65537; k++) cnt[k] += cnt[k - 1];\n      stage = 2;\n    } else {\n      final e = math.min(n, i + 65536);\n      for (; i < e; i++) dst[cnt[(keys[src[i]] >> s) & 0xFFFF]++] = src[i];\n      if (i == n) { final t = src; src = dst; dst = t; cnt.fillRange(0, 65537, 0); pass++; stage = 0; i = 0; }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe result is in `src`. Run it as a phase with the count `() => _rx!.done ? _unit : _unit + 1`.\n\n(2) Split `_sortSweep` into two phases:\n- First, a chunked collection of the `_tp != 0` ids into Int32List(_nT), in 65,536-triangle units. Its last unit sets `_rx = _Radix(Int32List.sublistView(buf, 0, m), _tk)` and `_keep = Uint8List(_nT)`.\n- Then the radix phase. `_cullChunk` takes `_sweep = _rx!.src` once the radix is done.\n\n(3) Split `_finish` the same way:\n- The k1/k2 key loop over _nT, in chunks.\n- The two radix sorts (k2, then k1) as _Radix phases.\n- The remap fill and mark, and the nv numbering over _nV, in chunks (keep nv as a field so the order is unchanged).\n- One unit that allocates the output arrays.\n- The compaction over _nV, in chunks.\n- The tri/sector fill over `asc`, in chunks.\n- One unit that constructs the LanderMesh.\n\nAlso cut the sky and cull chunks from 4096 to 2048 triangles. That is safe because both loops run in sequence and keep no state per chunk.\n\n(4) In widgets/lander_view.dart:232, call `runSliced(..., rows: 1, sliceMs: 4)`. A slice is then at most about 4 ms plus one unit of about 4 ms or less, which is within the 8 ms rule.\n\n(5) Optional, native only: build with `Isolate.run(() => buildLanderMesh(...))` through the existing dem_load_io/web conditional-import split, so Android does no mesh work on the UI thread at all. Keep the sliced path for web.\n\n(6) Add a test on the shipped DEM that asserts the largest single step(1) time stays under the budget.\n\nNote: chunking fixes the jank but not the roughly 2.3 s total, which is 10× the 250 ms native budget. Most of that cost comes from the 750 × 750 far patch (about 2.2M triangles, about 97% of them culled). Fixing the total needs either the isolate on native or a smaller or decimated far patch.", "evidence": "I could not refute the claim. I reproduced it on the shipped DEM (dem_400m.bin.gz, size 2280, 400 m), and the numbers break the spec's own budget. docs/landers_view_spec.md §8 says: native ≤ 250 ms CPU total, web ≤ 1.2 s, no slice > 8 ms of work. Note: the worktree HEAD is ecd4cd4, which descends from 6ee08b1; the code is as the claim describes.\n\nCode facts:\n- widgets/lander_view.dart:232 calls `runSliced((r) => _meshKey != key || w.step(r), pause: Future.delayed(zero), rows: 8, sliceMs: 8)`.\n- runSliced (state/light_engine.dart:155) only checks the stopwatch after each step(rows). So a slice lasts at least one whole step(8).\n- core/lander_view.dart:438/440 make `_sortSweep()` and `_finish()` one unit each. The class doc comment at line 361 even says so: \"the dmin sort and the final compaction are one unit each\".\n- `_sortSweep` scans all _nT triangles twice and radix-sorts every terrain triangle (two 16-bit LSD passes).\n- `_finish` loops over _nT for the keys and over _nV (about 1.3M) for the remap and compaction. It also allocates k1/k2 as Uint32List(_nT).\n- The far patch is landerFarPx(800 m) = 750 px, so it has 749² × 4 ≈ 2.2M triangles. Of these, SR1 keeps only 61,716 and the custom spot keeps 13,701.\n\nHow I measured: a throwaway flutter test (VM JIT) and a `dart compile exe` AOT build of the same pure-core code, run 3 times (JIT) and 2 times (AOT) per spot. Both have since been deleted.\n- SR1, JIT, warm: total 2276 to 2471 ms over 675 step(8) calls. 81 to 95 calls took over 8 ms. The longest step was 181 to 192 ms, and 13 to 44 slices ran over 16 ms. On the cold first run the longest step was 663 ms.\n- SR1, one unit at a time:\n  - JIT: unit #4884 (_sortSweep) 139 ms, unit #5399 (_finish) 52 ms.\n  - AOT: _sortSweep 134 to 182 ms, _finish 55 to 66 ms, total 2.47 to 2.96 s. A few cull chunks also ran 8 to 10.6 ms.\n- Custom spot (-80.05, 1.44):\n  - JIT: total 1.92 to 2.09 s, longest step 104 to 124 ms. One unit at a time, _sortSweep took 111 ms and _finish 26 ms.\n  - AOT: _sortSweep 144 to 159 ms, _finish 50 to 52 ms, total 2.0 to 2.5 s.\n\nConclusions:\n- The total is about 10× the 250 ms native budget, and AOT is no faster.\n- The two single units block the UI thread for 50 to 180 ms on x64. That is several dropped frames, and it will be longer on ARM Android and in dart2js.\n- Every other step(8) runs past 8 ms (about 20 to 25 ms) because 8 far cell rows or 4096-triangle chunks run before the time check.\n- The 4 CPUs were shared with other jobs, but the ove"}, {"from": "performance review", "severity": "high", "title": "projectTriangles re-merges and re-sorts the visible sectors and allocates two list literals per triangle on every paint (~7 ms per frame vs a 2 ms budget)", "file": "app/lib/core/lander_view.dart", "line": 887, "failure_scenario": "LanderPainter.paint (widgets/lander_view.dart:951) calls projectTriangles(v, m, visibleSectors(v), proj) on every repaint, and every repaint means every cam.notifyListeners tick of a drag, the 400 ms turn animation or a fling. Each call copies the sector lists into a new Int32List, runs ids.sort() over 25-40k ids, and then allocates `[a, b, c]` twice per triangle in its inner loop. Measured on the SR1 mesh (34k vertices, 62k triangles) at 411x560 px, pitch -10: mean 4.6-6.8 ms per call at hfov 45 and 7.2-7.5 ms at hfov 90, worst 15 ms. The merge and sort alone take 3-5.6 ms. The same projection loop over a cached id list, without the list literals, takes 0.56 ms. Spec §3.7/§8 says the index list is rebuilt only when the sector set changes and projection must stay within 2 ms native / 6 ms web. On the web, with dart2js running 2-3x slower, dragging the view spends 15-20 ms per frame on projection alone, so frame time goes past 16 ms and a drag judders. Repaints caused by the time bar (shouldRepaint is true whenever colors, sun or earth change, i.e. on every play step) also re-sort and re-project even though the camera has not moved.", "suggested_fix": "Cache the merged, ordered triangle-id list in LanderProjection, keyed by the sector set: k-way merge the already-ascending sector lists, and rebuild only when visibleSectors changes. Replace the two `for (final q in [a, b, c])` loops with unrolled code for a, b and c. Keep xy/idx/batches cached and keyed by (yaw, pitch, hfov, w, h), so a repaint caused by a time step reuses the previous projection and only re-gathers colours.", "status": "confirmed", "verified_fix": "The smallest correct fix is in /home/user/lv-wt/app/lib/core/lander_view.dart only. It stays pure Dart and needs no widget change.\n\n**1. Cache the merged ids in `LanderProjection`, keyed by mesh and sector set.**\n\nAdd these fields:\n```dart\nLanderMesh? _idsMesh; List<int> _idsKey = const []; Int32List _ids = Int32List(0);\n```\n\nAt the top of `projectTriangles`:\n```dart\nif (!identical(out._idsMesh, m) || !_sameInts(out._idsKey, sectors)) {\n  out._ids = _mergeSectors(m, sectors);\n  out._idsMesh = m;\n  out._idsKey = List.of(sectors);\n}\nfinal ids = out._ids;\n```\n\n- `_sameInts` is a plain length check plus an element loop. Do not use `listEquals`, which is a Flutter import.\n- `_mergeSectors` is a k-way merge of the already-ascending `m.sectors[s]` lists:\n  - Allocate `Int32List(total)` and an int position per list.\n  - For each output slot, take the smallest head and advance that list.\n- This drops the per-paint `ids.sort()`. A rebuild costs about 1 ms and happens only about every 10° of yaw. Concat-then-sort would also be correct, but it costs 3-7 ms per rebuild and breaks the §8 limit of 2 ms per index rebuild.\n\n**2. Unroll the two `for (final q in [a, b, c])` loops.**\n- Compute `fresh` as `(stamp[a] != cur ? 1 : 0) + (stamp[b] != cur ? 1 : 0) + (stamp[c] != cur ? 1 : 0)`.\n- Replace the second loop with a local `put(int q)` closure called as `put(a); put(b); put(c);`. `put` does the same stamp, local, vert, xy and idx writes as the current loop body.\n- This keeps the draw order and batching exactly the same. Its output was byte-identical to the current code in my test.\n\n**3. Optional: skip re-projection on time-bar repaints.**\n- Store the last `(yaw, pitch, hfov, w, h)`, the mesh and the triangle count in `LanderProjection`.\n- Return early when they are unchanged.\n- A time-bar repaint then only re-gathers colours in `_pass`. This saves the remaining 0.5-1.5 ms; it is not required to meet the budget.\n\n**Tests:** add a test in /home/user/lv-wt/app/test/core/lander_view_test.dart:\n- Project the same view twice, and project across a sector-set change.\n- Assert `xy`, `idx`, `vert` and `batches` equal those from a fresh `LanderProjection`.\n- Assert the ids stay ascending.", "evidence": "I could not refute it: the claim holds, and I reproduced it on the real SR1 mesh from the shipped DEM. Note that the worktree HEAD is ecd4cd4, not 6ee08b1; I reviewed the code as checked out there.\n\n**What the code does**\n- `projectTriangles` (/home/user/lv-wt/app/lib/core/lander_view.dart:876-946) rebuilds the triangle id list on every call: `Int32List(total)`, then `setRange` for each sector, then `ids.sort()`. It also allocates `[a, b, c]` twice per kept triangle (lines 920 and 929).\n- `LanderPainter.paint` (/home/user/lv-wt/app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart:951) calls `projectTriangles(v, m, visibleSectors(v), proj)` on every paint.\n- The painter uses `super(repaint: cam)` (line 712). `LanderCameraController.set` (lines 113-118) calls `notifyListeners()` on every yaw, pitch or hfov change, so every drag, turn or fling tick repaints.\n- `shouldRepaint` (line 1141) is true whenever `colors`, `sun` or `earth` change, so each time-bar step also re-merges, re-sorts and re-projects, even though the camera has not moved.\n- The spec, /home/user/lv-wt/docs/landers_view_spec.md, sets limits the code breaks:\n  - §3.7: \"The index list is rebuilt only when that sector set changes.\"\n  - §8: index rebuild ≤ 2 ms native; projection ≤ 2 ms native / 6 ms web.\n\n**Measurement**\nI ran a throwaway pure-Dart benchmark (now deleted) importing only `lib/core`, on the SR1 mesh built exactly as the app builds it: `buildLanderMesh` on `dem_400m.bin.gz`, giving 34,367 vertices and 61,716 triangles. View: 411x560 px, pitch -10, yaw swept 0-359 in 1° steps, 3 passes. The machine was shared, so the p95 and max figures are noisy.\n\n| hfov | Build | Current `projectTriangles` | Merge + sort alone | Fix, cached ids + unrolled (rebuilds included) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 45 (25,358 ids) | JIT | mean 4.49, p95 9.5, max 12.4 ms | mean 3.01 ms | mean 0.67 ms |\n| 45 (25,358 ids) | AOT | mean 5.62, max 12.8 ms | mean 4.11 ms | mean 0.91, max 3.0 ms |\n| 90 (36,950 ids) | JIT | mean 8.30, p95 16.8, max 21.4 ms | mean 5.88 ms | mean 1.17 ms |\n| 90 (36,950 ids) | AOT | mean 10.41, p95 21.3, max 24.1 ms | mean 7.62 ms | mean 1.59 ms |\n\nWith a cache hit, projection alone takes 0.55 ms at hfov 45 and 0.99 ms at hfov 90 (JIT); AOT was about 1.5x slower in this run.\n\n- **Fix gives identical output:** at both hfovs and all 360 yaws in the checked pass, the fixed path produced the same `xy`, `idx`, `vert`, batch count and triangle count as the current code (0 mismatches).\n- **Concrete trigger:** drag the "}, {"from": "performance review", "severity": "medium", "title": "A mesh build in flight is not cancelled on dispose: leaving lander mode keeps a multi-second UI-thread build (+104 MB transient) running, and re-entering starts a second one", "file": "app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart", "line": 232, "failure_scenario": "The runSliced stop predicate is `_meshKey != key || w.step(r)`. dispose() (line 200) only clears _meshes and never changes _meshKey, so the predicate stays false after the panel is gone. light_map.dart mounts LanderViewPanel only while kind == LightViewKind.lander. A user who opens the lander's view and switches back to the map within the 2+ s build (or toggles it twice) leaves the orphaned build running to the end, consuming UI-thread slices and the transient working set. I measured that working set with ProcessInfo.currentRss: RSS rose by 104 MB (164 to 268 MB) during one SR1 build, against the spec's ≤ 25 MB peak, because about 1.33M vertices (pixel plus centre vertices) carry Float64 az/el/dh and about 2.6M triangle slots plus radix buffers. Re-entering lander mode creates a new State that starts a second, concurrent build, so the UI-thread time doubles and the peak memory is about 200 MB transient. On Android low-RAM devices and in mobile browsers that risks the process being killed. The result is then thrown away (`!mounted`).", "suggested_fix": "Add a `_disposed` flag (or set `_meshKey = null`) in dispose, and include it in the runSliced predicate: `(r) => !mounted || _meshKey != key || w.step(r)`. Separately, shrink the transient set toward the 25 MB budget: Float32 for _az/_el/_dh, and build the fan centres per cell only where they are needed, or drop large phase arrays as soon as they are consumed.", "status": "not verified", "verified_fix": null, "evidence": null}, {"from": "performance review", "severity": "low", "title": "Recolour on each new light frame takes 7-8 ms for the 'both' layer (budget 3 ms native), and runs twice per time step on native", "file": "app/lib/state/lander_light.dart", "line": 156, "failure_scenario": "didUpdateWidget calls _recolor whenever the near frame's identity changes, i.e. on every play step or scrub. _recolor runs landerVertexColors and then landerPatternColors for each pattern of the layer, and each landerPatternColors call first runs landerPatternAlpha in full and then a second per-vertex pass that calls _source() (a record per vertex) and reads m.part/m.w through the object. Measured on the SR1 mesh with real computed frames: the base colours take 2.2-3.0 ms, the two patterns 4.2-5.2 ms, so 'both' costs about 7-8 ms per recolour in the VM, against spec §8's ≤ 3 ms native / 8 ms web. On native the far frame lands about 50-150 ms after the near one and triggers a second full recolour (plus _refreshText's meshProbe and setState) for the same moment, so each play step pays twice. On the web, where play steps are already UI-thread-bound by the light engine, the recolour plus the per-paint projection above makes every play step a frame of well over 16 ms.", "suggested_fix": "Fuse the pattern alpha and the pattern colour into one pass, compute the base and all pattern buffers in a single loop over vertices, and hoist the near/far source selection out of the loop (vertices are grouped by part, so loop per part range instead of calling _source per vertex). When only the far frame arrives, recolour only the part-2 vertices.", "status": "not verified", "verified_fix": null, "evidence": null}]

const STRICT = `YOUR BUDGET IS 150k AND IT IS STRICT: earlier agents ran out by reading whole files. Never read a whole file; grep -n, then sed -n at most 80 lines at a time (app/lib/widgets/lander_view.dart, light_map.dart ~2000 lines, app/lib/core/lander_view.dart ~1050, app/lib/state/lander_light.dart).`

phase('Verify')
const todo = REVIEWED.filter(f => f.status === 'not verified')
const verified = await parallel(todo.map((f, i) => () => agent(`${COMMON}

${STRICT.replace('150k', '70k')} Read-only (throwaway tests in ${REPO}/app/test/_rev_tmp_c${i}/, deleted afterwards). A ${f.from} agent claims: ${f.title} at ${f.file}:${f.line} (${f.severity}). Scenario: ${f.failure_scenario}. Suggested fix: ${f.suggested_fix}. Try to REFUTE it from the real code and data; reproduce where feasible. Default to refuted=true without a concrete trigger. If real, give the smallest correct fix.`, { label: `verify: ${f.title}`.slice(0, 70), phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT }).then(v => ({ ...f, verdict: v }))))
const confirmed = [
  ...REVIEWED.filter(f => f.status === 'confirmed').map(f => ({ ...f, verdict: { fix: f.verified_fix, evidence: f.evidence } })),
  ...verified.filter(Boolean).filter(f => f.verdict && !f.verdict.refuted),
]
log(`${confirmed.length} confirmed findings to fix`)

phase('Fix')
const fixes = []
for (const area of ['geometry review', 'performance review', 'ui review']) {
  const items = confirmed.filter(f => f.from === area)
  if (!items.length) continue
  fixes.push(await agent(`${COMMON}

${STRICT} Apply these confirmed problems in the lander's view (${area}), smallest correct change each, with a test where it makes sense; keep docs/landers_view_spec.md true where the build deliberately differs, and tick each fixed item in its section 14. Other agents fix the other areas before or after you; touch only what these need. Do not commit or push. Run in ONE call at the end: cd ${REPO}/app && flutter analyze && flutter test test/core/lander_view_test.dart test/state/lander_light_test.dart test/widget/lander_view_test.dart test/widget/light_map_test.dart > ${SCRATCH}/lv_fix_${area.split(' ')[0]}.log 2>&1; tail -3. Problems: ${JSON.stringify(items.map(f => ({ title: f.title, file: f.file, line: f.line, fix: f.verdict.fix, evidence: String(f.verdict.evidence || '').slice(0, 1000) })), null, 1)}`, { label: `fix: ${area} (${items.length})`, phase: 'Fix', schema: FIX_SCHEMA }))
}

phase('Look')
const shotPrompt = (round) => `${COMMON}

${STRICT} Regenerate the lander's-view screenshots: a throwaway flutter test under app/test/zz_tmp_landers_shots/ (pump the real app Light tab with the Lander segment, real data, fonts as in app/test/widget/light_test_fonts.dart, fakes installed inside the test body as lander_view_test.dart's installFakes does) for: MP1 sunlit with long shadows, MP1 at 2026-03-03 11:30 UTC (eclipse), a custom spot near 85°S, a moment with the Sun below the horizon, and MP1 in phone landscape (844x390); save PNGs with suffix _r${round} to ${SHOTS}/ and each one's 2D 'Around site' map as <name>_r${round}_map.png; delete the throwaway folder. Return the paths in screenshots.`
const looks = [], fixes2 = []
for (let round = 1; round <= 2; round++) {
  await agent(shotPrompt(round), { label: `screenshots round ${round}`, phase: 'Look', schema: RESULT })
  const look = await agent(`${COMMON}

Read-only. YOUR BUDGET IS 80k. You are the visual critic. Look (Read tool) at the lander's-view screenshots with suffix _r${round} in ${SHOTS}/ and at each one's 2D map partner (_map.png). For each: does the lit/shadowed terrain in 3D match the 2D map's lit area around the site; is the skyline plausible against the site's horizon; are the Sun/Earth markers where the status lines say; is anything mirrored, upside-down, clipped, overlapping, unlabelled, unreadable or invented? Report only problems you can point at in a named PNG; an empty list is fine.`, { label: `look: round ${round}`, phase: 'Look', schema: LOOK })
  looks.push(look)
  const real = ((look && look.problems) || []).filter(p => p.severity !== 'low')
  if (!real.length) break
  fixes2.push(await agent(`${COMMON}

${STRICT} Fix these visual problems in the lander's view, smallest correct change each, with a test where it makes sense. Do not commit or push. Run cd ${REPO}/app && flutter analyze && flutter test test/widget/lander_view_test.dart test/widget/light_map_test.dart in one call. Problems: ${JSON.stringify(real, null, 1)}`, { label: `fix: ${real.length} visual problems (round ${round})`, phase: 'Look', schema: FIX_SCHEMA }))
}

return { verified, confirmed: confirmed.map(f => f.title), fixes, looks, fixes2 }
